WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:04.440
Back in June at AI DevCon in London, we had a whole run of talks

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.040
about what happens to security when the agent has the keys.

3
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:13.200
Prompt injection, poisoned skills, and the awkward fact that most

4
00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.760
of the tools we've handed our agents come with no security controls whatsoever.

5
00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:23.120
Let's first hear from Joseph Katsioloudes of the GitHub Security Lab,

6
00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:26.520
who points out there's roughly one application security

7
00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:30.360
specialist for every 100 developers and argues

8
00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:35.240
we should stop saying shift left and start saying start left.

9
00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.640
Okay. Good morning everybody.

10
00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:40.240
Well, perfect.

11
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.440
Welcome to Code Security Reinvented.

12
00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.680
Navigating the era of artificial intelligence.

13
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:45.160
My goal

14
00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:49.360
today is to show you practical ways to use artificial intelligence for security

15
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:52.160
use cases. Whatever I show you, you can do.

16
00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.600
Also, in Claude Code, Codex, not just GitHub Copilot.

17
00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:57.640
Okay. Let's get started.

18
00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:59.400
Yeah.

19
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.480
180 million plus developers.

20
00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:04.920
A platform that builds and scales secure software.

21
00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:06.040
Specifically, my team

22
00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:09.240
is the GitHub Security Lab, which is a team of security experts

23
00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:12.840
with the mission to secure the open source software that we all rely on.

24
00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.600
And we do this through research, education and other activities.

25
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:21.600
For instance, this is some research from last year about bypassing Ruby-SAML.

26
00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:25.280
Just last week we showed how somebody

27
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.600
could exploit 7-Zip with a heap buffer overflow.

28
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:32.360
We have found and helped fix more than 1000 vulnerabilities,

29
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:36.720
900 plus of which have been given unique security identifiers.

30
00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:40.160
And the most important thing is that we help people fix those.

31
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.600
We don't just report them.

32
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:42.880
And this is important

33
00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.080
because, exactly, there is a gap between security and developers.

34
00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:51.400
If we try to quantify this gap, there is just one application

35
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:55.400
security specialist for every 100 software developers.

36
00:01:55.480 --> 00:02:01.080
This gap is the opportunity that we can help minimize with AI.

37
00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.760
However, if we are not equipped with the right knowledge,

38
00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:08.640
the gap maybe is going to widen or it's not going to close

39
00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.200
because we didn't maximize our potential there.

40
00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:12.920
And that's the point of this talk.

41
00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.160
I want to show you the pros and cons, the drawbacks of AI,

42
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:22.240
so you can make a human-in-the-loop use case of how you can use it responsibly.

43
00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:27.120
So this is the first statement: AI can help minimize the security gap.

44
00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.680
Let's see how. The first thing is writing safer code.

45
00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:34.560
For me this is very important because all my career so far, I'm

46
00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.720
hearing from senior leaders in cybersecurity about shifting left.

47
00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:44.960
The problem when you shift left is that you keep having a gap on the left.

48
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.400
The whole point and the opportunity here is to start

49
00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:52.160
left, and starting left means a lot of things.

50
00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:56.560
And of course it has to touch the developers

51
00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:59.560
how the code is being built in the first place.

52
00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.280
We also heard from Liran Tal, who does AI security research at Snyk.

53
00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:08.240
His team scanned around 4000 published skills.

54
00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:11.840
Roughly 1 in 7 had something wrong with it.

55
00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.200
Malware, suspicious downloads.

56
00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:15.680
Credential harvesting.

57
00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:18.720
Raise your hand

58
00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:22.920
if you added a skill to your agent anytime in the last few months.

59
00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.920
And keep it up.

60
00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:27.840
Okay? I just see some hands now.

61
00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.720
Keep it up. Keep it up.

62
00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:31.080
I want you to keep it up.

63
00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:34.360
Only now, if you actually review the skill

64
00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:37.360
and read the markdown.

65
00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.880
So many liars.

66
00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:41.000
Liars, all of you.

67
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:42.960
I'll show you why today.

68
00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:45.120
All right. Cool. We're good.

69
00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.240
So yeah, I'm at Snyk.

70
00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:50.880
I basically do a lot of developer advocacy, and I try to mix that up

71
00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:51.880
with security research.

72
00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:55.800
Over the last year or so, things like AI security research,

73
00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:00.720
which also goes to account to things like skills and MCP security.

74
00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:04.360
And we've done a bunch of research over the past few months,

75
00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:05.600
which I do want to talk about today,

76
00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:09.880
and it relates directly to skill security. So.

77
00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:12.160
At around

78
00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:16.840
4000 skills on ClawHub back in about

79
00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:20.640
January, February was the peak when we actually ran the scan.

80
00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:24.160
That means about 1 in 7.

81
00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:26.280
This is the 30%.

82
00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:30.360
1 in 7 of those skills had some kind of issue with it.

83
00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.840
So that would have been something like malware distribution, suspicious

84
00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:38.640
downloads, maybe credential harvesting, or just misuse,

85
00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:42.440
or just basically any kind of potential security vulnerabilities

86
00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:44.480
like Guy talked about in the opening keynote.

87
00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:47.480
So skills aren't just malware, they're also potentially

88
00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:51.800
having security vulnerabilities in them, just like regular code and other things.

89
00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:54.960
So everyone probably knows what a skill looks like today, right?

90
00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:56.400
That's kind of like this.

91
00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:57.640
There is a front

92
00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:01.400
matter that gives you instructions and sets the metadata.

93
00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:05.200
There is the body with more metadata, gives instructions to the agent,

94
00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:07.280
what to do and so on.

95
00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:10.280
But when you look at it there's like no sandboxing information.

96
00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:11.560
There are no permissions here.

97
00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:12.400
There's no other

98
00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:16.320
kind of security controls for the agent to know what to do with the skills.

99
00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:19.840
Oleg Šelajev is on the DevRel team at Docker

100
00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:23.720
and he brought a demo I would call genuinely alarming.

101
00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:27.720
His agent refuses to run a secret scanning skill,

102
00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:32.040
so he writes it in another language, then wraps it in a module.

103
00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:36.280
Then he clears the context and it runs the whole thing happily.

104
00:05:36.680 --> 00:05:40.200
So what you can see here is Claude running in auto mode, and I'm asking it

105
00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:44.080
to run this skill and it sensibly refuses.

106
00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:45.200
It says it's dangerous,

107
00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:48.200
I'm not going to do that, which is what auto mode is supposed to do.

108
00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:53.240
Then we continue the session and ask it to rewrite that in Python code.

109
00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:57.600
So take the skill, write me Python code to do this, and it complies

110
00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:01.320
because this is a benign request to write a piece of software.

111
00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:02.360
So it does that.

112
00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:05.360
And I say, can you please run this for me?

113
00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:10.760
And it says, I will not run this for you because of the security implications

114
00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:15.560
so I'm not going to run this as part of my ethical operating practices.

115
00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:20.640
But we go further and I ask it to put it in a Python module,

116
00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:24.520
because it can read the actual Python file that is supposed to run,

117
00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:27.440
but there are dependencies and dependencies of dependencies.

118
00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:31.200
So we put it in the module and it puts it in the module.

119
00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:34.320
And it says, I'm going to put it in a module for you.

120
00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:37.840
And at that moment I had a brilliant idea to rewrite it in Rust.

121
00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.240
So it only gets the binary.

122
00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:42.560
So it cannot actually peek under the hood what it's running.

123
00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:44.600
And then Claude says, I'm

124
00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:47.600
not going to rewrite that in Rust because you're clearly malicious.

125
00:06:47.960 --> 00:06:50.400
So it has some sense. But then

126
00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:53.640
it writes it as a Python module.

127
00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:56.640
I try to run it, it refuses.

128
00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:00.320
I do /clear so it loses all the context.

129
00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:04.960
And after that, it's very happily running this Python script that pulls the module

130
00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:09.280
dependency to do a complete security audit of my machine.

131
00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:14.280
And you can see that, that there are 20 keys in my SSH directory.

132
00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:15.360
That was a few months ago.

133
00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:20.240
After that, seeing that, I migrated them all into 1Password or something.

134
00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:23.520
There are cloud credentials and API keys in the history,

135
00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:27.880
and that the agent also can write to the code directory.

136
00:07:27.880 --> 00:07:30.600
So all consistent. So machine is completely vulnerable.

137
00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:31.040
Right.

138
00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:35.920
If I just run my agent naked on my machine. And to close, back to Lamis

139
00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:39.800
Mukta at Anthropic, this time on the unglamorous engineering

140
00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:42.800
that makes agent memory safe to run in production.

141
00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:45.720
Versioning. So you can roll a bad memory back.

142
00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:48.920
And a way to stop two agents writing over each other.

143
00:07:49.240 --> 00:07:50.960
Because memories can go stale.

144
00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:54.080
And they can also be poisoned on purpose.

145
00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:56.960
The final problem is that memories can go stale.

146
00:07:56.960 --> 00:08:01.400
Of course, something that was relevant in the past might not be relevant today.

147
00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:05.880
Or maybe it was written incorrectly or even maliciously injected

148
00:08:05.880 --> 00:08:10.560
by someone trying to prompt inject your agents to write bad things to memory.

149
00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:15.920
So you have to have a lot of guardrails in place to make sure that these nice

150
00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:19.000
autonomous memory systems actually work in production.

151
00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:25.200
And so I'm going to talk through a couple of key principles

152
00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:29.520
that we use when designing memory systems in production to make sure that we do

153
00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:33.640
get to use all of those nice effects that we've talked, that we've spoken about.

154
00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:37.160
So the very first thing is versioning.

155
00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:38.440
So when you're designing

156
00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:41.840
any kind of memory system, you need to be able to store versions

157
00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:45.720
to keep track of what's going on, to allow you to roll back

158
00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:49.280
should you need to, if a new update isn't particularly good.

159
00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:50.880
Additionally,

160
00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:54.640
you probably want to think about what context was this update based on.

161
00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:55.960
So which agent session?

162
00:08:55.960 --> 00:09:00.040
Which transcript resulted in me wanting to make this update?

163
00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:03.200
And additionally,

164
00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:06.240
like you might want to track like who did it, which agent, which human, etc.

165
00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:07.600
etc.

166
00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:09.120
So this is really important.

167
00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:11.440
The second thing is concurrency.

168
00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:14.600
So we've talked about okay, what happens when I deploy thousands of agents

169
00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:17.600
all working off the same memory system.

170
00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:20.200
And the solution that we've adopted

171
00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:23.240
here is to have this hashing system.

172
00:09:23.240 --> 00:09:26.560
So when an agent decides that it wants to write

173
00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:29.560
an update to a memory, it takes a hash.

174
00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:31.240
It then drafts its edit.

175
00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:35.520
And then before it writes the update, it takes another hash.

176
00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:38.920
If those two things do not match, then the agent cannot write it

177
00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:42.120
because it means that some update was made in the meantime.

178
00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:45.080
And in order to handle that, the agent re-reads

179
00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:49.000
the memory, drafts its new update, and then tries to commit this again.

180
00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.960
So these are the kinds of just engineering practices that allow you to scale

181
00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:57.880
multiple agent architectures, scale memory to these kinds of architectures.

182
00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:00.040
Thanks for watching.

183
00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:04.680
Be sure to join us for the next AI DevCon this November in New York City.

